{"choices":[{"finish_reason":"stop","index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"My last letter had brought me as far as Elberfeld; from there it went straight across Westphalia via Arnsberg and Arolsen to Kassel. From Arnsberg, instead of a fast mail, an ordinary old post wagon departed, and it was the last one—the next time it would be an express wagon—I had to endure that last one; my hope of still going to Eisenach on Sunday could therefore not be fulfilled; I had to spend the night in Cassel, where we arrived only in the afternoon, rested, went to the theater for half an hour—Goethe’s Egmont—and only yesterday, on Monday, drove to Eisenach with a hired coachman—through forest valleys, a country not exactly pleasant. What we saw of Westphalia, the Wupper valley, then other valleys, the area around Arnsberg was more charming. Here I was no longer in France nor in the beautiful Netherlands, neither as to the regions nor the inns; the latter I noticed because of the good appetite I have; partly because for a few days there have been no good ones to be had, and partly because the coachmen led me to those that in their opinion or according to their interest were good, i.e., bad. I spent the night in Eisenach; in the morning before daybreak, so that I saw nothing of the town (for I had arrived at night), I drove out with a single-horse carriage in thick fog, which was only conquered by the sun towards Gotha; now it went in fine weather, from friendly Gotha to Erfurt, where I sought out Mr. v. Griesheim, but did not find him, though I met his brother, and paid my compliments to his mother, a truly dear, cultivated, and intelligent lady, who was not unknown to me. In the evening—with the setting sun—I then arrived here yesterday. So, after some arranging, I proceeded to the goal of this detour—the old revered friend. The house was illuminated; the Grand Duke had announced himself for tea; however, I had my arrival reported for the time being. Goethe received me in the friendliest and most heartfelt manner; I had many things to tell him; after half an hour the old Grand Duke came;—but I must still add a main point—that I found Zelter with Goethe besides Riemer. Goethe presented me to the gracious Lord, to whom I set myself on the sofa—I believe I even sat to his right—he asked about Paris—he is somewhat deaf. So the evening passed (Zelter and Riemer wisely sat in the adjoining room) as well as it could with the old gentleman, in conversation, until half past ten o’clock. Goethe stood by the whole time; I gradually noticed that the gentleman was somewhat deaf, and that when it became quiet with speaking, one should not try to entertain him, but wait until something occurs to him again—otherwise everything went quite unembarrassed; I had to endure being nailed to my sofa for a couple of hours. The Grand Duke had recommended that I see his botanical garden in Belvedere; I drove with Zelter this morning—Goethe had had his carriage ready for it—at ten o’clock out; they are in fact very large, extensive grounds; the Duke is himself a great botanist—there are beautiful specimens of plants to be seen there—we two were of course not connoisseurs enough to properly appreciate everything—we were back here at noon; I paid my visit to Mr. and Mrs. v. Schwendler, where I naturally heard much regret that you are not there and that I am separated from Goethe in"}}],"created":1795219196,"model":"qwen","system_fingerprint":"b11429-d81235049","object":"chat.completion","usage":{"completion_tokens":756,"prompt_tokens":942,"total_tokens":1698,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":0}},"id":"chatcmpl-k8F0W4ZnBRwQyYpgKubsDqqkEWgxfLF1","timings":{"cache_n":0,"prompt_n":942,"prompt_ms":908.568,"prompt_per_token_ms":0.9645095541401274,"prompt_per_second":1036.796365269303,"predicted_n":756,"predicted_ms":17827.404,"predicted_per_token_ms":23.61245562913907,"predicted_per_second":42.35052955550904}}